# Claude Agent SDK Demo

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:claude_agent_sdk, path: "."},
  {:pythonx, "~> 0.4.2"},
  {:kino_pythonx, "~> 0.1.0"}
])
```

## Introduction

This LiveBook demonstrates the **Claude Agent SDK for Elixir** - an SDK for interacting with Claude through the Claude Code CLI.

> **Prerequisites**: You need the Claude Code CLI installed:
> 
> ```bash
> npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
> ```

## Quick Start: Simple Query

The easiest way to get a response from Claude:

```elixir
# Get a simple text response
ClaudeAgent.query_text("What is the capital of France?")
```

## Get Full Result with Metadata

Get detailed information about the response including cost and duration:

```elixir
result = ClaudeAgent.query_result("What is 2 + 2?")

IO.puts("""
Answer: #{result.result}
Cost: $#{result.total_cost_usd}
Duration: #{result.duration_ms}ms
Session ID: #{result.session_id}
""")

result
```

## Streaming Messages

Stream all messages as they arrive for more control:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.Messages.{AssistantMessage, SystemMessage, ResultMessage}
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.ContentBlocks.TextBlock

ClaudeAgent.query("Explain pattern matching in Elixir in 2 sentences.")
|> Enum.each(fn
  %SystemMessage{} = msg ->
    IO.puts("📍 System: #{msg.subtype}")

  %AssistantMessage{content: blocks} ->
    Enum.each(blocks, fn
      %TextBlock{text: text} -> IO.puts("🤖 #{text}")
      other -> IO.inspect(other, label: "Content Block")
    end)

  %ResultMessage{} = msg ->
    IO.puts("\n✅ Done! Cost: $#{msg.total_cost_usd}")

  other ->
    IO.inspect(other, label: "Message")
end)
```

## Using Options

Configure Claude's behavior with options:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Options

opts = %Options{
  system_prompt: "You are a helpful Elixir expert. Be concise.",
  max_turns: 3
}

ClaudeAgent.query_text(
  "What's the difference between `=` and `<-` in Elixir?",
  opts
)
```

## Interactive Client: Multi-turn Conversations

The `Client` module enables stateful, multi-turn conversations where Claude remembers context:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Client
alias ClaudeAgent.Options
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.Messages.{AssistantMessage, ResultMessage}
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.ContentBlocks.TextBlock

# Helper to extract text from messages
extract_text = fn messages ->
  messages
  |> Enum.filter(&match?(%AssistantMessage{}, &1))
  |> Enum.flat_map(& &1.content)
  |> Enum.filter(&match?(%TextBlock{}, &1))
  |> Enum.map(& &1.text)
  |> Enum.join(" ")
end

# Start client with bypass permissions (for demo purposes)
{:ok, client} = Client.start_link(%Options{permission_mode: :bypass_permissions})

IO.puts("Client started!")
client
```

### First Message

```elixir
# Send first message
:ok = Client.query(client, "My name is Alice and I'm learning Elixir. Remember this.")

messages = client |> Client.receive_response() |> Enum.to_list()
IO.puts("📝 Response:")
IO.puts(extract_text.(messages))

messages
```

### Follow-up Message (Claude Remembers Context)

```elixir
# Follow-up - Claude should remember the name
:ok = Client.query(client, "What is my name and what am I learning?")

messages = client |> Client.receive_response() |> Enum.to_list()
IO.puts("📝 Response:")
IO.puts(extract_text.(messages))

messages
```

### Another Follow-up

```elixir
# Ask a related question
:ok = Client.query(client, "Give me one tip for what I'm learning, keeping it brief.")

messages = client |> Client.receive_response() |> Enum.to_list()
IO.puts("📝 Response:")
IO.puts(extract_text.(messages))

messages
```

### Cleanup

```elixir
# Always disconnect when done
Client.disconnect(client)
IO.puts("✅ Client disconnected")
```

## Tool Usage Example

Claude can use tools like reading files. Here's an example with tool permissions:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Options

opts = %Options{
  allowed_tools: ["Read", "Glob"],
  permission_mode: :bypass_permissions,
  cwd: File.cwd!()
}

# Ask Claude to read a file
ClaudeAgent.query_text(
  "Read the mix.exs file and tell me the package name and version.",
  opts
)
```

## Inspect Tool Usage in Detail

See exactly what tools Claude uses:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.Messages.AssistantMessage
alias ClaudeAgent.Types.ContentBlocks.{TextBlock, ToolUseBlock, ToolResultBlock}

opts = %Options{
  allowed_tools: ["Read"],
  permission_mode: :bypass_permissions,
  cwd: File.cwd!()
}

ClaudeAgent.query("What's in the README.md? Summarize in one sentence.", opts)
|> Enum.each(fn
  %AssistantMessage{content: blocks} ->
    Enum.each(blocks, fn
      %TextBlock{text: text} ->
        IO.puts("💬 Text: #{text}")

      %ToolUseBlock{name: name, input: input} ->
        IO.puts("🔧 Tool Use: #{name}")
        IO.inspect(input, label: "   Input", pretty: true)

      %ToolResultBlock{tool_use_id: id, content: content} ->
        preview = if is_binary(content), do: String.slice(content, 0, 200), else: inspect(content)
        IO.puts("📋 Tool Result (#{id}):")
        IO.puts("   #{preview}...")

      other ->
        IO.inspect(other, label: "Block")
    end)

  _other ->
    :ok
end)
```

## Server Info

Get information about the Claude Code server capabilities:

```elixir
{:ok, client} = Client.start_link(%Options{permission_mode: :bypass_permissions})

# Must connect first (or call query) before server_info is available
:ok = Client.connect(client)

{:ok, info} = Client.get_server_info(client)

IO.puts("Server Capabilities:")
IO.inspect(info, pretty: true)

Client.disconnect(client)
info
```

## Error Handling

The SDK provides specific error types:

```elixir
alias ClaudeAgent.Errors.{CLINotFoundError, ProcessError, JSONDecodeError}

# Example of error handling (this won't fail if CLI is installed)
try do
  ClaudeAgent.query_text("Hello!")
rescue
  e in CLINotFoundError ->
    IO.puts("❌ CLI not found: #{e.message}")
    if e.cli_path, do: IO.puts("   Searched: #{e.cli_path}")

  e in ProcessError ->
    IO.puts("❌ Process error: #{e.message}")
    if e.exit_code, do: IO.puts("   Exit code: #{e.exit_code}")
    if e.stderr, do: IO.puts("   Stderr: #{e.stderr}")

  e in JSONDecodeError ->
    IO.puts("❌ JSON decode error: #{e.message}")
end
```

## Message Types Reference

Here's a quick reference of the message types you'll encounter:

| Type               | Description                            |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| `SystemMessage`    | System events (init, auth status)      |
| `AssistantMessage` | Claude's responses with content blocks |
| `UserMessage`      | User messages (when echoed back)       |
| `ResultMessage`    | Final result with cost/duration        |

Content blocks within `AssistantMessage`:

| Block Type        | Description                       |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `TextBlock`       | Plain text response               |
| `ThinkingBlock`   | Claude's reasoning (when enabled) |
| `ToolUseBlock`    | Tool invocation details           |
| `ToolResultBlock` | Result from tool execution        |

## Cleanup

Make sure to clean up any resources:

```elixir
# If you have any running clients, disconnect them
# Client.disconnect(client)
IO.puts("🧹 Cleanup complete!")
```
